.TH od 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
od \- octal and hexadecimal dump
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B od
[\fB\-bcdhovx\fR] [\fIfile\fR] [[\fB+\fR]\fIoffset\fR[\fB.\fR][\fBb\fR]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B od
writes the contents of
.I file
in one or more formats, one line of output for every sixteen bytes of
input, with the offset in octal at the left.  With no
.IR file ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
The default format is
.BR \-o .
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-b
Bytes in octal.
.TP
.B \-c
Bytes as characters, with escapes for the ones that will not print.
.TP
.B \-d
Words in unsigned decimal.
.TP
.B \-h
Words in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-o
Words in octal.
.TP
.B \-x
Words in hexadecimal.
.TP
.B \-v
Show every line.  Without it, runs of identical lines are printed once
followed by an asterisk.
.SH THE OFFSET
An offset given after the file name says where to start.  It is octal
unless followed by a dot, which makes it decimal; a trailing
.B b
multiplies it by 512.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# od -c file
# od -bc file
# od -x prog.bc 1000.
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cat (1),
.BR cmp (1)
